query:
I commute by bike in summer and also carry snacks in a hiking pack. I want a sweet that can ride through a roughly 90-degree afternoon without turning into a leak or a wrapper-stuck mess. The frozen candidates are an $11.79 M&M'S 38-ounce milk-chocolate party bag rated 73 percent over twelve reviews, a $29.99 Skittles tub described as ninety individually wrapped fun-size packets with no posted review, a $7.96 thirteen-ounce Jolly Rancher sour hard-candy bag rated 85 percent over twelve reviews, and a $19.95 six-count Charleston Chew selection described as 1.87-ounce nougat bars with a chocolatey coating and no posted review. Which, if any, can I reasonably carry in a pannier or hiking pack, and what would I need to verify or test before choosing?

Please audit the exact offers and the available background under these boundaries:

- keep each exact title SKU price rating review count pack mass form flavor and manufacturer statement at frozen seller assertion scope;
- do not treat MMS colorful shell trail mix or everywhere marketing as a measured hot weather survival claim;
- do not treat chocolatey coating wording as proof of compound chocolate fat identity or a higher melting threshold;
- do not treat hard candy identity as proof that an exact piece cannot soften stick absorb moisture or damage its wrapper;
- do not assign a product melting point crystal form glass transition or humidity threshold from a general material page;
- treat shelf stable as a sealed room temperature storage category not a guarantee of texture wrapper integrity or safety after uncontrolled heat;
- keep each community statement at its author container product scale time and condition scope not as a product or route result;
- verify current physical product identity complete ingredients allergens lot net quantity pack count storage directions and seal integrity before any trial;
- exclude or defer any exact item with unresolved label storage filling allergen or package integrity requirements;
- measure the actual pannier and backpack temperature humidity sun shade duration and position profile before claiming a 90 degree exposure;
- trial only small quantities in identical leakproof secondary containment with matched start temperature route duration and bag position;
- test uncooled and ice pack or insulated conditions as separate predeclared factors and do not mix them into a brand effect;
- predeclare no leak no wrapper adhesion shape retention texture acceptability consumption packaging waste and cooling burden thresholds;
- treat trial results as local to the tested lot route bag position weather and duration not a universal brand winner; and
- choose only the lowest total trip cost and carry burden exact path that passes current label safety packaging and matched route exposure gates otherwise use a proven control or defer.

Give me a practical research and carry-selection process that will:

1. build an exact offer matrix for price rating review count form pack mass packaging and unresolved label fields;
2. separate crystalline melting polymorphism and compound coating concepts from exact SKU thresholds;
3. separate hard candy glass transition and humidity mechanisms from a heatproof claim;
4. separate passive packaging active cooling and route exposure effects from product identity;
5. verify current physical labels storage directions allergens lot seal integrity and any perishable filling before trial;
6. instrument the actual pannier and backpack profiles and run a small matched contained exposure trial; and
7. choose the lowest total trip cost exact snack path passing current label safety packaging and matched route exposure gates or use a proven control or defer.

Explain what each seller page, material concept and community report can and cannot establish. Distinguish classic crystalline melting from sugar-glass softening and humidity-related stickiness without inventing exact thresholds. Spell out the bag measurements, physical-package checks, containment, cooling controls and predeclared outcomes I would need. End with a procedure I could actually follow, while avoiding universal heat-resistance, health, food-safety or brand-winner claims.
